Force TDEUniqueApplication to the no-fork startup path on substrate.

KUniqueApplication::start() defaults to the fork() launch path (s_nofork =
!--fork).  In that path the application fork()s, the child registers its DCOP
name, and the parent exits.  On substrate the forked daemon shares the parent's
DCOP connection; when the parent exits the dcopserver tears that shared
connection down, so the daemon registers and then loses its connection
silently.  kded is launched this way by tdeinit and never answers tdeinit's
--check handshake, so starttde's auto-launch cascade collapses (relaunch ->
duplicate dcopserver -> "Communication error with launcher. Exiting!" /
error 111).

The no-fork branch registers DCOP in-process and is reliable (kded --nofork
works standalone), so force it unconditionally.  Verified: with this change
`starttde` reports `Launched KDED ... result = 0` (was the communication
failure).

--- a/tdecore/kuniqueapplication.cpp
+++ b/tdecore/kuniqueapplication.cpp
@@ -100,6 +100,14 @@
   s_nofork = true;
 #else
   TDECmdLineArgs *args = TDECmdLineArgs::parsedArgs("kuniqueapp");
-  s_nofork = !args->isSet("fork");
+  /* substrate: force the nofork path.  The fork() launch path below leaves the
+   * forked daemon sharing the parent's DCOP connection; when the parent exits
+   * the dcopserver tears that shared connection down, so the daemon registers
+   * its DCOP name and then loses the connection silently.  kded then never
+   * answers tdeinit's --check handshake, and starttde's auto-launch cascade
+   * collapses (relaunch -> duplicate dcopserver -> "communication error" 111).
+   * The nofork branch registers DCOP in-process and is reliable (kded --nofork
+   * works standalone), so take it unconditionally. */
+  s_nofork = true;
   delete args;
 #endif
